## Attribution

The ratchet design in this package is adapted, with permission of its MIT
licence, from the `code_health/` directory of PortfolioOptimisers.jl by Daniel
Celis Garza:

    Copyright (c) Daniel Celis Garza
    https://github.com/dcelisgarza/PortfolioOptimisers.jl
    MIT License

Specifically adapted from that work: binding complexity on the maximum over a
file's definitions rather than the sum; binding coverage on the miss count
rather than the percentage; binding JET on a reviewed count with named
dismissals; keeping thresholds out of the pass rule; checking parseability
separately; pairing renames on a row's full number set, and the argument that
equal numbers make the pairing safe; splitting measured baselines from
hand-written rulings; the `rulings.toml` section structure; the unmeasured-path
assertion; deepest-repository-frame attribution; pinning the JET load set in
provenance; and the check / refresh / refresh --accept-rise verbs.

Adapted in a second pass, after reading his implementation rather than only its
rationale comments: the set-equality rule that makes a file with no baseline row
a failure; attributing a coverage miss to the top-level definition holding it,
including the `Core.@doc` unwrapping that makes a documented definition name
itself; holding a coverage exemption to its exact count in both directions, and
the argument that a claim below the truth is a leak the file total cannot see;
excluding `commit` from the provenance comparison; taking the file list from
`git ls-files` rather than a directory walk, and why; the `::error` annotations
and step-summary table; the refresh artifact, so a contributor fixes a red gate
by committing a downloaded file; the ordered remedy routes that name a refresh
last; and capping the scheduled job on the open-issue count rather than per run,
with the whole-token title match that decides what is already filed.

Several docstrings in this package paraphrase the rationale comments in that
work closely enough to be considered derived text, not independent authorship.

The approach was described at
https://discourse.julialang.org/t/idiomatic-julia-code-in-ai-generated-code/139183/4
